course: dictionary
lesson: 4411
title: When the mind sees things that are not there
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe what happens inside the mind. To hallucinate means to see, hear or feel things that are not really there. Someone with a high fever, an illness or lack of sleep might hallucinate and believe a shadow is a monster.
explain: Computers can hallucinate too, in an informal sense. When an AI chatbot makes up facts that sound true but are false, we say it hallucinated. «Bots can hallucinate facts, so every fact in a project must be checked; no volcano has erupted in London.» The clue is the second half: the sentence tells you the invented fact is wrong, so you must check.
explain: Ask yourself: what would fit here? Try your meaning in the sentence: «Bots can invent false facts, so every fact must be checked» still makes sense, so your meaning works. Hallucinate is a verb, so it changes: hallucinates, hallucinated, hallucinating.
fact: Hallucinate is a verb, so its forms are hallucinates, hallucinated and hallucinating.
fact: «Hallucinate» means to see, hear or feel things that are not really there.
fact: People may hallucinate with a high fever, an illness or lack of sleep.
fact: In computing, «hallucinate» means an AI chatbot makes up facts that sound true but are false.
fact: Always check facts from a chatbot against a trusted source.
exercise: «Bots can hallucinate facts, so every fact in a project must be checked.» What does hallucinate mean here? | Check every fact carefully | Speak very loudly | Forget facts quickly | *Make up facts that sound true but are false
exercise: A child with a high fever says she can see a dragon in the empty room. What is happening to her? | She is sleeping soundly | She is reading a map | She is planting a garden | *She may be hallucinating
exercise: Copy the word that is a form of hallucinate: «The AI hallucinated a source.» | the | AI | source | *hallucinated
exercise: Why does the sentence say «no volcano has erupted in London»? | To tell us London is famous | To describe a real volcano | *To show that a hallucinated fact is false | To explain how volcanoes form
test: «The AI hallucinated a source that does not exist.» What does this tell us? | The chatbot is ill and needs rest | The source is very old | The chatbot found the best source in the library | *The chatbot invented a source that sounds real but is false
test: What should you do with a fact a chatbot gives you? | Delete the whole project | *Check it against a trusted source before you use it | Believe it at once, because computers never make mistakes | Change the spelling of the fact
exam: Which sentence uses hallucinate in the computing sense? | The student could hallucinate the answer to every question | The fever made her hallucinate a happy dream | *The chatbot hallucinated a fact about a famous battle that never happened | The hallucinating boy saw birds in the sky
